# Pagination

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Pagination splits a long list, a table or a guide into pages, so each page is short enough to read and quick to load. Use numbered pages for many similar records. Use previous and next links for a guide that reads in order.

Home Assistant has no pagination element. Its own tables scroll and do not page. Pagination here is a Civica pattern, and the one place Civica pages data is the energy chart table.

This page covers:

- Pagination
  - Civica Pagination Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

There are two forms:

- **Buttons in a table.** A Previous rows button and a Next rows button, with a line of text between them such as “Rows 1 to 100 of 250”. The table sits inside a “View data” disclosure.
- **Links in a list or guide.** A labelled navigation area with previous and next links that name their topic, or a numbered list of pages with the current page marked.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-pagination-pattern"><code>Civica Pagination Pattern</code></h3>

**Energy chart table.** The “View data” table under an energy chart has the columns Series, Period start, Value and Unit, and shows 100 rows to a page. The text between the buttons says which rows you are looking at, for example “Rows 1 to 100 of 250”. The buttons are called Previous rows and Next rows. On the first page Previous rows is disabled, and on the last page Next rows is disabled. When there are 100 rows or fewer, both buttons are hidden. If the data changes, the table goes back to page 1. See [Charts and tables](charts.html).

**Links.** For a guide or a route-based list, leave out the previous link on the first page and the next link on the last. Name the destination in each link, such as “Previous: Measurements”. For numbered pages, mark the current page with `aria-current="page"` and give each link a name such as “Page 2”. Put a single labelled navigation area on the page, or give each one its own label.

The pattern has no theme variables of its own. The Civica theme affects surrounding text and lines through `primary-color` for links and `divider-color` for borders.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Short pages load and read faster.
- Home Assistant’s own tables scroll, so a long table there has no page controls. The nearest alternatives are filters, the period controls on the History panel, or one scrollable table.
- Infinite scrolling can stop keyboard users reaching the end of a page, so do not use it.
- The table buttons change the rows on the same page. Link pagination changes the address, so Back and direct addresses work.
- Disabled buttons are less obvious than missing ones. The row text between the buttons is what tells people where they are.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Keep a short list on one page when that is easier to understand. Do not use pagination to advance a form, or to hide information that people need now.

When the page changes, keep the query, period, filters and sort. Keep the row count visible, and say how many pages there are. Keep the buttons in the same place on every page, and never move to another page automatically.

After a change that happens without a new page loading, announce the new row range and move focus somewhere predictable, without trapping it.

Check the first page, the last page, an empty result, a count that shrinks, the browser Back button and direct addresses. Use the [accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html) at 320 CSS pixels and in light and dark modes.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the navigation area is labelled and each control has a name.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose: a link names its destination.
- 2.4.3 Focus Order and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured: focus moves predictably.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: a completed page change is announced.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): controls are at least 24 CSS pixels. Measure your own controls before you claim it.
- 3.2.2 On Input: nothing changes page until someone chooses.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the controls wrap at 320 CSS pixels.

